{
  "dom_id": "step-q75268",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
  "worked_solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-{e}^{-0}=-1\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
